# feature-nilpy-thirdparty-libraries-as-targets — relative imports INSIDE a
# pulled .py MODULE, which is a different code path from the one
# test_nilpy_relative_import.npy covers.
#
# That test puts the relative imports in the MAIN PROGRAM, which reaches them
# through PyParseImportRun's leading run and always worked. A real package
# writes them in its `__init__.py`, and a pulled module's statements go through
# PyParseStatement instead — whose import gate required an IDENTIFIER one token
# past `from`, so a leading dot fell out of import handling entirely and the
# statement parser met a bare `from` and called it an undefined variable.
#
# webencodings/__init__.py:19 (`from .labels import LABELS`) is what found it,
# and it blocked ALL FOUR fetched Python corpora, because a package that does
# not use relative imports internally is the exception.
#
# The two spellings are both exercised here: `from .mod import names` and the
# bare `from . import mod` with qualified access.
#
# Note what is deliberately NOT asserted: a name IMPORTED into __init__.py is
# not re-exported to the package's own importers (flat unit scope), which is a
# separate defect with its own ticket. Only names this __init__.py DEFINES are
# read here.
from nilpy_relpkg import S, T

print("sum", S)
print("through-func", T)
